WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.060
Reliable loop design in n8n starts with a simple mental model: an item is a unit of data moving through the workflow.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:16.520
A trigger, database query, webhook, file reader, or API request may create one item, five items, or thousands of items.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:22.780
Each item carries its own JSON data, and downstream nodes receive the list of items from the previous step.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.640
In many workflows, that list is already the repetition mechanism.

5
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.740
The workflow does not need a visible loop just because there is more than one item.

6
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.060
Most n8n nodes are designed to process incoming items automatically.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:46.480
If a workflow loads five customer records and then sends a notification, the notification node can receive five items and perform the configured operation for each item.

8
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:53.460
The canvas may show one notification node, but the execution can still contain five notification operations.

9
00:00:53.560 --> 00:01:01.240
That distinction matters because the canvas shows the structure of the workflow, while the execution data shows how many items moved through that structure.

10
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.520
People with a programming background often reach for an explicit loop too early.

11
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:13.140
In source code, repeated work is commonly written with a for statement, a while statement, or a function called inside an iterator.

12
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.960
In n8n, repeated work may already be represented by multiple incoming items.

13
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.700
Adding a Loop Over Items node to force a familiar shape can make the workflow harder to read.

14
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:31.360
It also introduces loop state, return connections, completion paths, and new failure modes that did not exist in the simpler design.

15
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.360
A practical example makes the difference concrete.

16
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:41.660
Imagine a workflow that reads five customer records, formats a message for each record, and sends one update for each customer.

17
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.640
The correct design may be a straight path from the data source to the message node.

18
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.620
Each item carries its own customer name, destination address, and message fields.

19
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.160
The message node uses expressions that refer to the current item.

20
00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:04.300
During execution, n8n evaluates those expressions for each incoming item, so every customer is handled separately without drawing a connection back to an earlier node.

21
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.340
The Execute Once setting is the important exception to keep in mind.

22
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:15.880
When Execute Once is enabled for a node, the node processes only the first input item instead of processing every item it receives.

23
00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:25.500
That can be useful when a node should run a setup step, create one shared value, or perform an operation that must happen one time for the whole execution.

24
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.800
It should not be treated as a shortcut for performance, and it should not be enabled casually inside a workflow that is supposed to act on every item.

25
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.280
A careful review therefore begins in the execution data.

26
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:45.580
Count the items coming out of the source node, inspect a few representative JSON objects, and confirm that the downstream expressions refer to fields that exist on each item.

27
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:54.960
When the item count and shape already match the business action, the direct workflow is easier to explain and easier to recover than a manual loop built only from habit.

28
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.480
The first engineering rule is to inspect the input before choosing a loop pattern.

29
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:06.140
Look at the number of items, the fields on each item, and the operation each downstream node is expected to perform.

30
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.320
If ordinary item processing expresses the behavior, keep the workflow direct.

31
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:17.540
If a node offers native pagination or batching that exactly matches the API requirement, prefer that native feature.

32
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:28.600
Build an explicit loop only when the workflow needs controlled release of items, a repeated multi-node body, custom pacing, or a condition that changes as the workflow runs.
